 ## How it works
-The method estimates the gradient $\nabla V$ at a voxel center $\mathbf{p}_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0)$ by fitting a plane $f(x,y,z) = Ax + By + Cz + D$ to the density values of the 26 neighbors in a $3 \times 3 \times 3$ neighborhood centered at $\mathbf{p}_0$. The gradient is given by the coefficients $[A, B, C]^T$ [S3].
+The method estimates the gradient $\nabla V$ at a voxel center $\mathbf{p}_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0)$ by fitting a plane $f(x,y,z) = Ax + By + Cz + D$ to the density values of the 26 neighbors in a $3 \times 3 \times 3$ neighborhood centered at $\mathbf{p}_0$ [S3]. The gradient is given by the coefficients $[A, B, C]^T$ [S3].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input**: A 3D scalar volume $V$. For a target voxel at index $(i, j, k)$, the input is the set of 26 neighboring voxel values $v_k$ at positions $\mathbf{p}_k$ relative to the center, where $k \in \{1, \dots, 26\}$. The center voxel value is excluded from the regression sum in the standard implementation described by Neumann et al. [S3].
-*   **Output**: The gradient vector $\nabla V = [A, B, C]^T$ at the center voxel.
